ASoC: cs35l56: Take turns reading a shared speaker ID GPIO

On the Dell XPS 14 DA14260, all four CS35L57 amps read their speaker ID
from the same host GPIO, and they probe at the same time: two of them
log their supplies within the same millisecond. cs35l56_get_speaker_id()
requests the GPIO exclusively, reads it and puts it back, so an amp that
asks while another holds it gets -EBUSY. That isn't a deferral, so its
probe fails for good, and with one amp missing the SoundWire machine
driver never registers the sound card. It happened on one boot in six.

A mutex around the request, the read and the put makes the amps take
turns. It's held for microseconds, and only by amps whose speaker ID
comes from GPIOs.

diff -ruN a/sound/soc/codecs/cs35l56-shared.c b/sound/soc/codecs/cs35l56-shared.c
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/cs35l56-shared.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/cs35l56-shared.c
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include <linux/fs.h>
 #include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/kstrtox.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
@@ -1535,6 +1536,13 @@
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_NS_GPL(cs35l56_hw_init, "SND_SOC_CS35L56_SHARED");
 
+/*
+ * Amps that share one speaker-ID GPIO probe at the same time, and each
+ * requests the GPIO exclusively, so one that asks while another holds it
+ * would get -EBUSY and fail its probe. Take turns.
+ */
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(cs35l56_spkid_gpio_lock);
+
 int cs35l56_get_speaker_id(struct cs35l56_base *cs35l56_base)
 {
 	struct gpio_descs *descs;
@@ -1559,6 +1567,7 @@
 	}
 
 	/* Read the speaker type qualifier from the motherboard GPIOs */
+	guard(mutex)(&cs35l56_spkid_gpio_lock);
 	descs = gpiod_get_array_optional(cs35l56_base->dev, "spk-id", GPIOD_IN);
 	if (!descs) {
 		return -ENOENT;
